C ↔ G: C is an open curve, while G has a small hook/loop at the bottom. In some fonts, the curves blend, especially if the G's hook is subtle.
D ↔ O: D has a straight vertical left side with a curve, O is fully round. This is a classic OCR error (as you mentioned), particularly in bold or distorted plates.
Q ↔ O: Q is similar to O but with a tail/loop at the bottom. The main body can confuse systems if the tail is cropped or faint.
Straight/Vertical Similarities
E ↔ F: Both have vertical lines with horizontals, but E has three bars (top, middle, bottom), F has two (top and middle). Thin fonts make the middle bar hard to distinguish.
P ↔ R: P has a straight top and a loop, R adds a diagonal leg. The upper curves are very similar, leading to swaps.
U ↔ V: U is rounded at the bottom, V is pointed. In some views or low-res images, they look almost identical.
Angular/Diagonal Similarities
K ↔ X: Both have crossed diagonals, but K has a vertical stem on the left, X is symmetric. Angular fonts can make them interchangeable.
M ↔ N: M has an extra peak in the middle, N is simpler. In wide fonts, the shapes overlap, especially if the middle peak is shallow.
W ↔ M: W is like two V's, M is like two N's. The peaks can blur together in OCR.
Y ↔ V: Y has a tail below the V-shape, V is symmetric. If the tail is short or cut off, they confuse.


